    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Triumph TF-E Bikes Get Even More Enduro With Updates for the 2027 Model Year\n2027 GMC Sierra HD Drops a 555-HP New Duramax V8 Under Its Hood, It'll Set You Back At Least $68K\n21 photos\nPhoto: GMC\nAmerican carmaker GMC is not one to come under the spotlight all that often, as for years now it has been content to copy, to different degrees, whatever parent company GM has up its sleeve for sister brand Chevrolet.\nThe same continues to be the case with the 2027 Sierra HD pickup truck, which mirrors many of the fittings and changes of the 2027 Chevrolet Silverado HD . Yet, as announced at the end of last week, there are some particularities of the model that finally make the brand worthy of a story of its own.\nBefore getting into what the changes are, it's important to note that the 2027 GMC Sierra HD is in no way visually different than the current version. Connoisseurs, however, will be able to instantly spot the new variant by the sound it makes in one of its versions.\nThe truck is offered by GMC in this new form with the good-old gasoline 6.6-liter V8, enhanced a bit to be able to develop 412 horsepower and 472 lb.-ft. of torque, with the latter number claimed to be best-in-class.\nPhoto: GMC The version that sounds different is the one that relies on a new diesel V8 to get going, a clean-sheet GM design. Part of the Duramax family, it displaces 8.3 liters and is rated at 555 hp (something, too, described as best in class) and 1,230 lb.-ft. of torque.\nBoth engine offerings for the 2027 GMC Sierra HD work in tandem with an Allison 10-speed transmission, and they are not the only mechanical changes made to the truck for the new model year.\nGMC throws into the mix rack-and-pinion electric power steering, something that's not at all common in this segment. The new setup replaces the previous hydraulic-assisted hardware offered.\nThe braking system of the pickup truck has been enhanced as well, with the fitting of a revised eBoost braking system that's a premiere in this segment as well. The setup consists of larger 14.6-inch front rotors and four-piston front calipers.\nPhoto: GMC GMC tinkered with the drive modes of the pickup truck to suit various needs and demands, and there are three major ones on deck, all modifying the performance and behavior of the engine, steering, braking, and chassis systems.\nThe Tow/Haul mode remains on deck for the 2027 model year, changing engine mapping, shifting, exhaust brake, grade-braking, EPS assist, and eBoost so that heavy loads can be hauled with ease.\nHow heavy the loads, you ask? GMC rates the Sierra HD at up to 20,000 pounds of towing capacity in standard configuration, and almost double that, 38,000 pounds, when featuring a max trailering gooseneck setup.\nThe truck's Off-Road mode is on deck to tweak ABS, traction control, and stability control so that the wheel is allowed more slip on loose surfaces. Finally, there is a new Terrain Mode, something the Sierra never used before.\nPhoto: GM Terrain Mode is only available on the AT4X model (more on trim levels later in this story), and it translates into the automatic application of braking power when the driver lifts their foot off the accelerator in 4HI or 4LO. It's a feature that should allow one-pedal control on steep grades and technical obstacles.\nAs said, the exterior of the truck is in no way different from what it used to be, but people might get a sense of novelty inside, especially when it comes to the technology aspects of driving the thing.\nWith the dashboard dominated by two screens, a 13.4-inch one for the infotainment system and a 12.3-inch instrument cluster, the Sierra now throws in 14 camera views designed to help with hitching, lane changes, reversing, and trailer monitoring, among others. An in-vehicle trailering app and trailer tire pressure and temperature monitoring are also thrown into the mix.\nI mentioned the trim levels for the Sierra HD, and here they are, six of them for both gasoline and diesel variants, and a seventh exclusive to the diesel model. The gas-powered track costs at least $57,495 in the entry-level Pro variant, and starts at $89,195 for the most gifted AT4X.\nPhoto: GM The diesel offering opens with the Pro as well, but this time the starting price is significantly higher, at $68,895. The top-of-the-range is no longer the AT4X, but the diesel-exclusive Denali Ultimate, which will set people back $99,395.\nThe mighty ZR2 package remains on the table as well, with all its crazy extras: a 1.5-inch factory-installed lift, larger Multimatic DSSV dampers, 35-inch MT tires, and aluminum skid plates, among others. GMC also offers, for the first time, a Trail Boss package that throws in Z71 stuff: a suspension package, an Autotrac 2-speed transfer case, a Z71 skid plate, and BFGoodrich All-Terrain tires.\nGMC said the order books for the pickup truck should open soon, especially seeing how it plans to have the 2027 models on dealers' lots as soon as December 2026.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nGMC Sierra HD gmc sierra pickup truck\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Tiny Cargo Trailer Turned 4-Season Off-Grid Mobile Home Is the Build You Have to See",
